<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muslim conquest of Transoxiana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/673">673</a>–<a href="/years/751">751</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> was the gradual conquest of the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> and Jaxartes rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> by the Umayyad and early Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a>, from the 670s to the mid-8th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> between 705 and 715, but Sogdian and Türgesh resistance lasted into the 740s; the victory over the Tang at Talas (751) secured the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-transoxiana.jpg" alt="Muslim conquest of Transoxiana" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer: The State Hermitage Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabs_besieging_the_city_of_Samarkand,_captured_in_722_CE,_Palace_of_Devastich_(706-722),_Penjikent_mural.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> in 642 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and with the fall of Marw to Abd Allah ibn Amir in 651 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>'s border reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>. The land beyond the river, mā wara al-nahr to the Arabs, was divided into many small principalities. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> local shads, many of them Turkic chieftains, owed nominal allegiance to the Yabghu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a>. Sogdia, an Iranian-speaking land with its own script, was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, whose ruler bore the title ikhshid; Khwarizm was ruled by the Khwarizmshah, and Ustrushana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a> and Fergana lay further north and east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first Arab campaigns were mostly raids for booty and tribute, launched every year from a small garrison at Marw. Under Ziyad ibn Abi Sufyan's government of the east, his deputy al-Hakam ibn Amr al-Ghifari led Arab armies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan">Chaghaniyan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a> then halted expansion for a generation, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>'s Arab settlers fought among themselves and Abd Allah ibn Khazim al-Sulami held the province until 691.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a>, backed by al-Hajjaj, consolidated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and recruited large bodies of native troops, converts and non-Muslims alike. In 707 he took Numushkat and Ramithna and defeated a coalition from Fergana and Sogdiana, and in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> he conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. In 719 local rulers asked the Chinese and the Türgesh for help, and from 720 the Türgesh attacked while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> rose. The governor Sa'id ibn Amr al-Harashi suppressed the revolt harshly; the rebel leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divashtich">Divashtich</a>, captured at his fortress on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a>, was crucified. Fergana was lost, and in 717 a Tang army had defeated an Arab, Tibetan and Türgesh attack at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Conversion was slow under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, who treated local non-Muslims as second-class subjects and did not encourage them to convert, so that few Sogdian commoners became Muslims. In the Abbasid period non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> gained equal status on conversion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The region conquered then covers all or part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, Tajikistan, Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muslim conquest of Transoxiana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
